\title{Structural Theory of One-Variable Max-Plus\\ Tropical Polynomial Functions}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-06-24}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop the elementary structural theory of the one-variable max-plus tropical polynomial function over the real numbers,
\[
\tropPoly_c(x) = \max_{0 \le i \le d}\bigl(c_i + i\,x\bigr),
\]
where $c = (c_0, \dots, c_d)$ is a tuple of real coefficients indexed by $i \in \{0, 1, \dots, d\}$. Building on a single reusable primitive --- the finite maximum of a real-valued function on a nonempty finite index set, together with its attainment and upper-bound characterization --- we establish the full suite of first-order structural properties of $\tropPoly_c$: each monomial is a pointwise lower bound; the maximum is attained at some index for every input; an upper bound holds iff it holds monomial-by-monomial; the function is monotone non-decreasing; it is convex in the Jensen sense; the leading (highest-slope) monomial dominates pointwise under a local dominance hypothesis and, more strongly, dominates for all inputs beyond any threshold at which it dominates; and the degree-one and degree-two cases admit explicit closed forms as nested binary maxima. A central methodological theme is that every structural theorem is obtained by a single uniform schema --- \emph{reduce a property of the envelope to the same property of each affine constituent, then observe the constituents satisfy it trivially}. We discuss the resulting proof-strategy template, numerical illustrations, algorithmic consequences, and connections to convex analysis, optimization, and piecewise-linear neural networks.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Tropical mathematics replaces the field operations $(+, \times)$ of ordinary arithmetic with the \emph{max-plus semiring} operations $(\max, +)$: ``addition'' is taking the maximum, and ``multiplication'' is ordinary addition. Under this dictionary, an ordinary monomial $c_i x^i$ becomes the affine expression $c_i + i\,x$, and the sum of monomials becomes their pointwise maximum. The image of a degree-$d$ polynomial is therefore a finite maximum of $d+1$ affine functions whose slopes are the exponents $0, 1, \dots, d$.

Such functions are ubiquitous: they are value functions of finite-horizon dynamic programs in the $(\max,+)$ algebra; they describe shortest- and longest-path costs; and, in machine learning, a single maxout unit and the ReLU activation are precisely degree-one tropical polynomials. The geometry of tropical polynomial functions --- piecewise linearity, convexity, and the eventual dominance of the steepest term --- is the geometry that makes these applications tractable.

This paper presents a complete, self-contained elementary theory of the one-variable case, with precise statements and proof sketches for every structural property, and an emphasis on the recurring \emph{proof strategy}: a higher-order schema reducing a statement about the upper envelope of affine functions to the same statement about each affine constituent. All results stated below have been formally verified.

\section{The finite-maximum primitive}

Let $n \in \N$ and let $f : \{0, 1, \dots, n\} \to \R$ be a real-valued function on the nonempty finite index set of size $n+1$.

\begin{definition}[Finite maximum]
Define $\finMax(f) = \max_{0 \le i \le n} f(i)$, the maximum of $f$ over the nonempty finite index set.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Upper bound, \texttt{le\_finMax}]
For every index $i$, $\;f(i) \le \finMax(f).$
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$f(i)$ is one of the finitely many values over which the maximum is taken, so it cannot exceed it.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Attainment, \texttt{exists\_finMax\_eq}]
There exists an index $i$ with $\finMax(f) = f(i)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
A maximum over a nonempty finite set is achieved at one of its members; choose that member.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Upper-bound characterization, \texttt{finMax\_le\_iff}]
For every $y \in \R$,
\[
\finMax(f) \le y \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad \forall i,\; f(i) \le y.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\Rightarrow$) $f(i) \le \finMax(f) \le y$. ($\Leftarrow$) If $y$ bounds every value, it bounds their maximum, since the maximum equals some $f(i_0) \le y$.
\end{proof}

The last lemma is the pivotal tool: it converts any upper-bound goal about the maximum into a family of upper-bound goals about the individual values.

\section{The tropical polynomial and its first-order properties}

\begin{definition}[Tropical polynomial]
Given a degree $d \in \N$ and coefficients $c : \{0,\dots,d\} \to \R$, define
\[
\tropPoly_c(x) = \finMax\bigl(i \mapsto c_i + i\,x\bigr) = \max_{0 \le i \le d}\bigl(c_i + i\,x\bigr), \qquad x \in \R.
\]
Each term $m_i(x) = c_i + i\,x$ is the \emph{$i$-th monomial line}, affine of slope $i$ and intercept $c_i$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Monomial lower bound, \texttt{tropPoly\_monomial\_le}]
For every $i$ and $x$, $\;c_i + i\,x \le \tropPoly_c(x).$
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Direct instance of the upper-bound lemma with $f(i) = c_i + i\,x$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Attainment, \texttt{tropPoly\_eq\_monomial}]
For every $x$ there exists $i$ with $\tropPoly_c(x) = c_i + i\,x$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Direct instance of the attainment lemma.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Upper-bound characterization, \texttt{tropPoly\_le\_iff}]
For all $x, y$,
\[
\tropPoly_c(x) \le y \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad \forall i,\; c_i + i\,x \le y.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Direct instance of the upper-bound characterization lemma.
\end{proof}

\section{Monotonicity}

\begin{theorem}[Monotonicity, \texttt{tropPoly\_mono}]
If $x \le y$ then $\tropPoly_c(x) \le \tropPoly_c(y)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By the upper-bound characterization it suffices to show $c_i + i\,x \le \tropPoly_c(y)$ for each $i$. Since $i \ge 0$ and $x \le y$, $c_i + i\,x \le c_i + i\,y \le \tropPoly_c(y)$ by the monomial lower bound.
\end{proof}

\section{Convexity}

\begin{theorem}[Convexity, Jensen form, \texttt{tropPoly\_convex}]
For all $x, y, t \in \R$ with $0 \le t \le 1$,
\[
\tropPoly_c\bigl(t x + (1-t) y\bigr) \;\le\; t\,\tropPoly_c(x) + (1-t)\,\tropPoly_c(y).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Fix $i$. Affinity gives the exact split
\[
c_i + i\bigl(t x + (1-t)y\bigr) = t\bigl(c_i + i\,x\bigr) + (1-t)\bigl(c_i + i\,y\bigr).
\]
Bound each piece by the monomial lower bound and use $t, 1-t \ge 0$ to obtain
$\le t\,\tropPoly_c(x) + (1-t)\,\tropPoly_c(y)$. The upper-bound characterization then lifts the bound from each $m_i$ to the maximum.
\end{proof}

\section{Leading-term dominance}

Write $m_d(x) = c_d + d\,x$ for the leading monomial, of maximal slope $d$.

\begin{theorem}[Pointwise dominance, \texttt{tropPoly\_eq\_leading}]
Fix $x$. If $c_i + i\,x \le c_d + d\,x$ for all $i$, then $\tropPoly_c(x) = c_d + d\,x$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Antisymmetry. The upper-bound characterization with $y = c_d + d\,x$ gives $\tropPoly_c(x) \le c_d + d\,x$; the monomial lower bound at $i=d$ gives the reverse.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Threshold dominance, \texttt{tropPoly\_eq\_leading\_threshold}]
Fix $T$. If $c_i + i\,T \le c_d + d\,T$ for all $i$, then for every $x \ge T$, $\tropPoly_c(x) = c_d + d\,x$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By the pointwise dominance theorem it suffices to verify the hypothesis at $x$. Decompose
$c_i + i\,x = (c_i + i\,T) + i(x-T)$ and $c_d + d\,x = (c_d + d\,T) + d(x-T)$. The threshold hypothesis controls the first bracket; since $i \le d$ and $x - T \ge 0$, $i(x-T) \le d(x-T)$. Adding yields $c_i + i\,x \le c_d + d\,x$.
\end{proof}

\section{Explicit low-degree expansions}

\begin{theorem}[Degree one, \texttt{tropPoly\_deg1}]
For $c = (c_0, c_1)$ and all $x$, $\tropPoly_c(x) = \max(c_0,\; c_1 + x)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Antisymmetry: ($\le$) the upper-bound characterization checks the two lines $c_0$ and $c_1 + x$ against the binary max; ($\ge$) each branch is a monomial value, hence $\le \tropPoly_c(x)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Degree two, \texttt{tropPoly\_deg2}]
For $c = (c_0, c_1, c_2)$ and all $x$,
\[
\tropPoly_c(x) = \max\bigl(c_0,\; \max(c_1 + x,\; c_2 + 2x)\bigr).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Identical structure over the three slopes $0,1,2$: upper bound checks each monomial value against the nested max; lower bound bounds each branch by $\tropPoly_c$.
\end{proof}

\section{The proof-strategy schema}

Abstracting the common pattern yields a reusable schema for upper envelopes of structured families. Let $g(x) = \max_i m_i(x)$. To prove $g$ satisfies an upper-bound-shaped property $P$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Reduce.} Use $g \le y \iff \forall i,\; m_i \le y$ to replace the goal about $g$ with goals about each $m_i$.
\item \textbf{Discharge per constituent.} Prove the reduced goal for each $m_i$ using shared structure (affinity, nonnegative slope, slope ordering $i \le d$).
\item \textbf{Reassemble.} For equalities, pair the reduced upper bound with attainment ($m_{i_0} \le g$) and conclude by antisymmetry.
\end{enumerate}
Every theorem in Sections 4--7 is an instance of this schema.

\section{Algorithms}

\textbf{Evaluate / argmax.} Scan $c_i + i\,x$ tracking the running maximum and its argument; $O(d)$, correct by attainment.

\textbf{Leading-term threshold.} For each $i < d$ the leading line overtakes line $i$ at $T_i = (c_i - c_d)/(d-i)$; set $T = \max_i T_i$. Correct by threshold dominance; $O(d)$.

\textbf{Corner enumeration.} The corners of the convex graph are the vertices of the lower convex hull of the points $(i, -c_i)$, computable in $O(d \log d)$; attainment identifies active monomials. This underlies a tropical fundamental theorem of algebra.

\section{Applications and future work}

Tropical polynomials are $(\max,+)$ value functions (monotonicity and convexity give well-behaved optimization landscapes); maxout and ReLU units are degree-one tropical polynomials; and corners are tropical roots whose count with multiplicity equals the degree. Routing everything through the finite-maximum primitive makes the index set incidental, enabling extensions: a tropical fundamental theorem of algebra; Legendre--Fenchel duality between coefficients and the graph; multivariate max-plus polynomials; continuity and $d$-Lipschitz bounds; and verified maxout/ReLU network layers.

\section{Conclusion}

From one primitive --- the finite maximum with its attainment and upper-bound characterization --- we obtained the complete first-order structural theory of one-variable max-plus tropical polynomials. Beyond the individual theorems, the recurring envelope-inheritance schema is itself the deliverable: a reusable higher-order template deriving properties of an envelope from the shared structure of its affine constituents.

\end{document}

